{
 "ep": 12,
 "total": 140,
 "title": "PostgreSQL Versions and Release Types",
 "phase": "Foundations",
 "lines": [
  {
   "say": "Can you upgrade PostgreSQL by changing one number?",
   "cap": "Upgrade *PostgreSQL* by changing *one* *number?*"
  },
  {
   "say": "This is episode twelve of the PostgreSQL DBA series. Today: major and minor releases, and upgrade planning.",
   "cap": "Episode 12: *major* vs *minor,* and upgrade *planning.*",
   "cue": "upgrade"
  },
  {
   "say": "The answer depends on which number changes. A version like eighteen point seven has two parts.",
   "cap": "Version *18.7* has *two* parts.",
   "cue": "two"
  },
  {
   "say": "The first number is the major version. It brings new features, about once a year.",
   "cap": "*18* is the major. New *features,* about *yearly.*",
   "cue": "features"
  },
  {
   "say": "The second number is the minor release. It carries bug fixes and security fixes, at least once every three months.",
   "cap": "*.7* is the minor. *Fixes,* at least every *3* months.",
   "cue": "security"
  },
  {
   "say": "This rule started with PostgreSQL ten. Before that, in nine point five point three, the major version was nine point five.",
   "cap": "Before *10:* in *9.5.3,* the major was *9.5.*",
   "cue": "Before"
  },
  {
   "say": "Check what you run. SHOW server_version gives the full version, and server_version_num gives one number for scripts.",
   "cap": "*SHOW* *server_version* and *server_version_num.*",
   "cue": "number"
  },
  {
   "say": "In the data directory, the PG_VERSION file holds only the major version: sixteen. Output trimmed.",
   "cap": "*PG_VERSION* holds only the *major:* 16.",
   "cue": "sixteen"
  },
  {
   "say": "This box runs sixteen point fifteen. Sixteen point sixteen came out on November twelfth, so it's one minor behind.",
   "cap": "*16.15* here. *16.16* is out: one minor *behind.*",
   "cue": "behind"
  },
  {
   "say": "A minor upgrade is simple. Minor releases never change the storage format. Stop the server, replace the binaries, restart.",
   "cap": "Minor upgrade: *stop,* *replace* *binaries,* *restart.*",
   "cue": "Stop"
  },
  {
   "say": "The data directory stays the same. Read the release notes first: some fixes need extra steps.",
   "cap": "Read the *release* *notes:* some need *extra* steps.",
   "cue": "release"
  },
  {
   "say": "The PostgreSQL project recommends that you always run the current minor release of your major version.",
   "cap": "Run the *current* *minor* of your major.",
   "cue": "recommends"
  },
  {
   "say": "A major upgrade is different. The internal storage format may change, so you cannot just swap the binaries.",
   "cap": "Major upgrade: storage format *may* *change.*",
   "cue": "swap"
  },
  {
   "say": "The docs list three ways: dump and restore with pg_dumpall, the faster pg_upgrade, or replication.",
   "cap": "*pg_dumpall,* *pg_upgrade,* or *replication.*",
   "cue": "replication"
  },
  {
   "say": "Before the real run, pg_upgrade with the check option tests both clusters without changing any data.",
   "cap": "*pg_upgrade --check:* checks, changes *no* data.",
   "cue": "check"
  },
  {
   "say": "You can jump several majors at once, but read the Migration section of every release in between.",
   "cap": "Skipping majors? Read every *Migration* section.",
   "cue": "Migration"
  },
  {
   "say": "Each major version is supported for five years. After that comes one final minor release, then end of life.",
   "cap": "*5* years of support, then *end* *of* *life.*",
   "cue": "five"
  },
  {
   "say": "This is happening now. PostgreSQL fourteen got its final release on November twelfth, twenty twenty six.",
   "cap": "*PostgreSQL 14:* final release on *Nov 12, 2026.*",
   "cue": "final"
  },
  {
   "say": "Before a new major ships, there are beta and release candidate builds for testing. PostgreSQL nineteen is at that stage now.",
   "cap": "*Beta* and *RC* builds are for *testing.*",
   "cue": "beta"
  },
  {
   "say": "Common mistake: treating minor updates as optional. They fix security issues and data corruption problems.",
   "cap": "Mistake: *skipping* minors. They fix *security* bugs.",
   "cue": "security"
  },
  {
   "say": "DBA takeaway: a common practice is to track versions and end of life dates, apply minors regularly, and test majors on a copy.",
   "cap": "*Track* versions. Apply *minors.* *Test* majors first.",
   "cue": "track"
  },
  {
   "say": "Recap: first number major, second minor. Minors swap binaries. Majors need pg_upgrade or a dump. Five years of support.",
   "cap": "Recap: *major,* *minor,* *upgrade* path, *5* years.",
   "cue": "Minor"
  },
  {
   "say": "Next up: installing PostgreSQL on Linux with packages. Follow so you don't miss it, and send this to a friend who's learning PostgreSQL.",
   "cap": "Next up: *install* on *Linux.* *Follow* and share."
  }
 ],
